[{:db/id 17592186192685 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5c38f1db-70a0-5329-a7b6-a10067496307" "question-ad24a909-ebd3-5c3e-8ab7-2f58fa308bd4" "question-3ccb0497-0675-55df-af7f-417ef72622bf" "question-2725c5ee-cb05-5659-94a6-debecd1e3f50" "question-8b570f89-5894-581a-a9f1-343151732b64"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192390 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample where $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10},$ you're given that there are $7$ successes. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta.$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0, 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $7$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(10-7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192795 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4f0ef3b3-16ee-5e79-a46a-c889944b927a" "question-ca066a36-cc0e-5545-b489-303cc36ed2ab" "question-a772fd34-b53c-57c2-a042-bacdd3f39061" "question-aac7d9dd-e1f6-55a0-8d3e-a363440ea2a6" "question-39034469-ded2-572d-9811-0121a992e366"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192519 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, X_3$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim B(5,\\theta)$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\nx_1 = 4, \\qquad x_2 = 1, \\qquad x_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nFind the log-likelihood function of this sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{5}{x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{5-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1, \\ldots, 5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 3$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x},\\,x=0,1,…,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}})) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum_ {i=1}^n x_i.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta)$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) =  \\ln\\left( \\prod_{i=1}^n {5 \\choose x_i}\\right) + S\\ln \\theta + (5n - S) \\ln(1-\\theta).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{1}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =125,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS = 4 + 1 + 1 = 6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(125)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5\\cdot 3-6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡125+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192796 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d978fa3e-6005-512f-876c-9fe43876b042" "question-766020c7-f3e3-5fda-b856-3ebb3916a4d5" "question-9585d3de-f669-5f4a-af08-1394e7187c1e" "question-feffea6d-5c4c-51cd-843c-44b1eafaf816" "question-680ba03b-6073-56c3-bf2a-b6b3fb6a93d2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192520 :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\theta)$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta.$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\nx_1 = 3,\\qquad x_2=4,\\qquad x_3 = 1.\n$$\n\nFind the log-likelihood function of the sample." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta.$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =3!\\cdot 4!\\cdot 1!=6\\cdot 24\\cdot 1=144, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3+4+1=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-3\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡144.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-5c38f1db-70a0-5329-a7b6-a10067496307" :question/id #uuid "5c38f1db-70a0-5329-a7b6-a10067496307" :question/math-academy-id "q-159869" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is an I.I.D random sample where ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{7},$ you're given that there are $3$ successes. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=7$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $3$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(7-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1edc6d3b-02d6-5897-ac02-ea834c0ed54b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1edc6d3b-02d6-5897-ac02-ea834c0ed54b" :answer-field/id #uuid "1edc6d3b-02d6-5897-ac02-ea834c0ed54b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-af6ffbd3-2b40-5b17-b106-49656d990b48" :answer-field/choices ["answer-af6ffbd3-2b40-5b17-b106-49656d990b48" "answer-ab107af7-aaf1-5009-9d41-de7830103788" "answer-0ec71840-3b63-5e08-83f4-28715450f86f" "answer-a9e80966-cc23-561a-973b-d44c3ce7fdd8" "answer-28bbbbae-0703-537d-bd9f-e05b52fb2181"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af6ffbd3-2b40-5b17-b106-49656d990b48" :answer/id #uuid "af6ffbd3-2b40-5b17-b106-49656d990b48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab107af7-aaf1-5009-9d41-de7830103788" :answer/id #uuid "ab107af7-aaf1-5009-9d41-de7830103788" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ec71840-3b63-5e08-83f4-28715450f86f" :answer/id #uuid "0ec71840-3b63-5e08-83f4-28715450f86f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9e80966-cc23-561a-973b-d44c3ce7fdd8" :answer/id #uuid "a9e80966-cc23-561a-973b-d44c3ce7fdd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28bbbbae-0703-537d-bd9f-e05b52fb2181" :answer/id #uuid "28bbbbae-0703-537d-bd9f-e05b52fb2181" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ad24a909-ebd3-5c3e-8ab7-2f58fa308bd4" :question/id #uuid "ad24a909-ebd3-5c3e-8ab7-2f58fa308bd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159866" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample where ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{8},$ you're given that there are $2$ successes. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=8$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $2$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(8-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a59cc903-f571-50a1-ad80-90fcdfc59f04"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a59cc903-f571-50a1-ad80-90fcdfc59f04" :answer-field/id #uuid "a59cc903-f571-50a1-ad80-90fcdfc59f04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-71f36fef-75c1-5d34-b448-6f30e0603675" :answer-field/choices ["answer-71f36fef-75c1-5d34-b448-6f30e0603675" "answer-19e67e58-5ddb-5b74-a0d2-698fee6ad1c0" "answer-98968f4e-5ace-53ce-a359-4e1a517d779f" "answer-81f4874e-1f7a-5935-ba6d-878da913cd14" "answer-59f4f668-a1de-5235-8b1b-3fad5eb76860"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71f36fef-75c1-5d34-b448-6f30e0603675" :answer/id #uuid "71f36fef-75c1-5d34-b448-6f30e0603675" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19e67e58-5ddb-5b74-a0d2-698fee6ad1c0" :answer/id #uuid "19e67e58-5ddb-5b74-a0d2-698fee6ad1c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98968f4e-5ace-53ce-a359-4e1a517d779f" :answer/id #uuid "98968f4e-5ace-53ce-a359-4e1a517d779f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81f4874e-1f7a-5935-ba6d-878da913cd14" :answer/id #uuid "81f4874e-1f7a-5935-ba6d-878da913cd14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59f4f668-a1de-5235-8b1b-3fad5eb76860" :answer/id #uuid "59f4f668-a1de-5235-8b1b-3fad5eb76860" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3ccb0497-0675-55df-af7f-417ef72622bf" :question/id #uuid "3ccb0497-0675-55df-af7f-417ef72622bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-159862" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ is an I.I.D random sample where ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{9},$ you're given that there are $4$ successes. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=9$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $4$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(9-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3639fc0c-c46e-5b4e-981f-14a079f619dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3639fc0c-c46e-5b4e-981f-14a079f619dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "3639fc0c-c46e-5b4e-981f-14a079f619dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46a90b6f-a526-572c-bfb0-1374c7be8a9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46a90b6f-a526-572c-bfb0-1374c7be8a9b" "answer-86fc2b48-30b7-5fd0-abef-0b4c9f1e7e2c" "answer-10976415-905c-5b20-9954-8ff44c1b310b" "answer-24194ef9-a9e7-5fee-bb39-b92b095742dd" "answer-0ce6261a-5852-5d54-864a-a9baf8857a31"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46a90b6f-a526-572c-bfb0-1374c7be8a9b" :answer/id #uuid "46a90b6f-a526-572c-bfb0-1374c7be8a9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86fc2b48-30b7-5fd0-abef-0b4c9f1e7e2c" :answer/id #uuid "86fc2b48-30b7-5fd0-abef-0b4c9f1e7e2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10976415-905c-5b20-9954-8ff44c1b310b" :answer/id #uuid "10976415-905c-5b20-9954-8ff44c1b310b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24194ef9-a9e7-5fee-bb39-b92b095742dd" :answer/id #uuid "24194ef9-a9e7-5fee-bb39-b92b095742dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ce6261a-5852-5d54-864a-a9baf8857a31" :answer/id #uuid "0ce6261a-5852-5d54-864a-a9baf8857a31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2725c5ee-cb05-5659-94a6-debecd1e3f50" :question/id #uuid "2725c5ee-cb05-5659-94a6-debecd1e3f50" :question/math-academy-id "q-159865" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is an I.I.D random sample where ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{15},$ you're given that there are $6$ successes. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=15$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $6$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(15-6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8cb889d0-1745-54fd-bdd2-e9fcc1868876"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8cb889d0-1745-54fd-bdd2-e9fcc1868876" :answer-field/id #uuid "8cb889d0-1745-54fd-bdd2-e9fcc1868876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c31b6268-de2b-5a73-81bd-e12567cb6d6b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f2336e8f-a8a8-5fad-bd47-216157df599f" "answer-19db2dcd-8b86-56ac-8ed1-048e517a286d" "answer-c31b6268-de2b-5a73-81bd-e12567cb6d6b" "answer-5f921750-5c6f-524a-9887-1d1723d9dd56" "answer-b4253f53-8c04-59f0-96ef-764121a8bcb4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2336e8f-a8a8-5fad-bd47-216157df599f" :answer/id #uuid "f2336e8f-a8a8-5fad-bd47-216157df599f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19db2dcd-8b86-56ac-8ed1-048e517a286d" :answer/id #uuid "19db2dcd-8b86-56ac-8ed1-048e517a286d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +15\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c31b6268-de2b-5a73-81bd-e12567cb6d6b" :answer/id #uuid "c31b6268-de2b-5a73-81bd-e12567cb6d6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f921750-5c6f-524a-9887-1d1723d9dd56" :answer/id #uuid "5f921750-5c6f-524a-9887-1d1723d9dd56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +15\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4253f53-8c04-59f0-96ef-764121a8bcb4" :answer/id #uuid "b4253f53-8c04-59f0-96ef-764121a8bcb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8b570f89-5894-581a-a9f1-343151732b64" :question/id #uuid "8b570f89-5894-581a-a9f1-343151732b64" :question/math-academy-id "q-159872" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample where ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ you're given that there are $2$ successes. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $2$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-322a7b1e-9588-5049-9d1f-487fc88d55f5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-322a7b1e-9588-5049-9d1f-487fc88d55f5" :answer-field/id #uuid "322a7b1e-9588-5049-9d1f-487fc88d55f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5c8f826-b802-50dd-804c-8d8527532124" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5c8f826-b802-50dd-804c-8d8527532124" "answer-9feb2eab-afdd-53e9-9b35-5ea893474079" "answer-11ae0523-db72-5ee3-ad6e-2aa05d8a483d" "answer-4e1b395c-5798-5603-a67e-3c9748205c63" "answer-15d7d119-3fa5-5cf9-8f20-28c31be19c4e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5c8f826-b802-50dd-804c-8d8527532124" :answer/id #uuid "e5c8f826-b802-50dd-804c-8d8527532124" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9feb2eab-afdd-53e9-9b35-5ea893474079" :answer/id #uuid "9feb2eab-afdd-53e9-9b35-5ea893474079" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11ae0523-db72-5ee3-ad6e-2aa05d8a483d" :answer/id #uuid "11ae0523-db72-5ee3-ad6e-2aa05d8a483d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e1b395c-5798-5603-a67e-3c9748205c63" :answer/id #uuid "4e1b395c-5798-5603-a67e-3c9748205c63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15d7d119-3fa5-5cf9-8f20-28c31be19c4e" :answer/id #uuid "15d7d119-3fa5-5cf9-8f20-28c31be19c4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f0ef3b3-16ee-5e79-a46a-c889944b927a" :question/id #uuid "4f0ef3b3-16ee-5e79-a46a-c889944b927a" :question/math-academy-id "q-159899" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(6,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=2,\\,{x}_{2}=5,\\,{x}_{3}=6.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=6$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{6-x},\\,x=0,1,…,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(6-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(6-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(6n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(6n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6}) \\\\  & =15\\cdot 6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =90,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=2+5+6=13.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(90)+13\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(6\\cdot 3-13)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(90)+13\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=90+13+5=108.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f05c59ec-e7dc-5ed7-9912-91abcb45f958"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f05c59ec-e7dc-5ed7-9912-91abcb45f958" :answer-field/id #uuid "f05c59ec-e7dc-5ed7-9912-91abcb45f958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d83abe2d-fdfb-5fae-8a4e-a8d17936fe98" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d83abe2d-fdfb-5fae-8a4e-a8d17936fe98" "answer-b134442c-59be-5c1f-b114-5798cbec57bd" "answer-19bf218d-db13-510f-87f9-55163f8181f4" "answer-f1441d25-5e60-5d9d-86dc-8d65cd6ea506" "answer-a4dc0644-cb20-5de3-8060-1da6759e8d4b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d83abe2d-fdfb-5fae-8a4e-a8d17936fe98" :answer/id #uuid "d83abe2d-fdfb-5fae-8a4e-a8d17936fe98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "108" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b134442c-59be-5c1f-b114-5798cbec57bd" :answer/id #uuid "b134442c-59be-5c1f-b114-5798cbec57bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "111" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19bf218d-db13-510f-87f9-55163f8181f4" :answer/id #uuid "19bf218d-db13-510f-87f9-55163f8181f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "89" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1441d25-5e60-5d9d-86dc-8d65cd6ea506" :answer/id #uuid "f1441d25-5e60-5d9d-86dc-8d65cd6ea506" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "94" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4dc0644-cb20-5de3-8060-1da6759e8d4b" :answer/id #uuid "a4dc0644-cb20-5de3-8060-1da6759e8d4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "97" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca066a36-cc0e-5545-b489-303cc36ed2ab" :question/id #uuid "ca066a36-cc0e-5545-b489-303cc36ed2ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-159896" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(4,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=3,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=4,\\,{x}_{6}=3.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=4$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x},\\,x=0,1,…,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{4}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{5}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{6}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{4})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =384,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=1+2+3+4+4+3=17.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(384)+17\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4\\cdot 6-17)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(384)+17\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=384+17+7=408.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99390f67-0220-5a26-b39c-6b99ee5db62a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99390f67-0220-5a26-b39c-6b99ee5db62a" :answer-field/id #uuid "99390f67-0220-5a26-b39c-6b99ee5db62a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c5fd872d-6b2f-555e-a4c0-ca31c54f4840" :answer-field/choices ["answer-34a2d95b-db4c-582b-b047-b5d2003afc20" "answer-02fad1fd-00fb-56f2-aaac-9305fb048262" "answer-97d020dd-5432-5417-b614-39723c95696e" "answer-c5fd872d-6b2f-555e-a4c0-ca31c54f4840" "answer-8d3c56d8-29ac-57f4-99fd-0dd9de89d063"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34a2d95b-db4c-582b-b047-b5d2003afc20" :answer/id #uuid "34a2d95b-db4c-582b-b047-b5d2003afc20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "386" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02fad1fd-00fb-56f2-aaac-9305fb048262" :answer/id #uuid "02fad1fd-00fb-56f2-aaac-9305fb048262" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "392" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97d020dd-5432-5417-b614-39723c95696e" :answer/id #uuid "97d020dd-5432-5417-b614-39723c95696e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "404" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5fd872d-6b2f-555e-a4c0-ca31c54f4840" :answer/id #uuid "c5fd872d-6b2f-555e-a4c0-ca31c54f4840" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "408" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d3c56d8-29ac-57f4-99fd-0dd9de89d063" :answer/id #uuid "8d3c56d8-29ac-57f4-99fd-0dd9de89d063" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "412" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a772fd34-b53c-57c2-a042-bacdd3f39061" :question/id #uuid "a772fd34-b53c-57c2-a042-bacdd3f39061" :question/math-academy-id "q-159893" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(7,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=6,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=2,\\,{x}_{4}=7.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{7-x},\\,x=0,1,…,7.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=7$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{7-x},\\,x=0,1,…,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{7-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{7-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(7-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(7-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(7n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{4}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(7n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{6})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{7}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 7\\cdot 21\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\,029,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=6+1+2+7=16.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1\\,029)+16\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(7\\cdot 4-16)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1\\,029+16\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +12\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1\\,029+16+12=1\\,057.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a5ebfda0-c699-5d31-9e1a-8c1a1852116a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a5ebfda0-c699-5d31-9e1a-8c1a1852116a" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5ebfda0-c699-5d31-9e1a-8c1a1852116a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-625d8f89-a7c7-5092-a6ad-ea8f0b72889f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f5a331b-060a-5a16-8b03-42447e8324e4" "answer-625d8f89-a7c7-5092-a6ad-ea8f0b72889f" "answer-3a98c440-68b6-575b-8ef4-c721edded79e" "answer-94519370-0c28-59dc-bc52-6f17306f1892" "answer-93637c4f-d62c-514b-a779-eb0ef0e60eb5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f5a331b-060a-5a16-8b03-42447e8324e4" :answer/id #uuid "4f5a331b-060a-5a16-8b03-42447e8324e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,029" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-625d8f89-a7c7-5092-a6ad-ea8f0b72889f" :answer/id #uuid "625d8f89-a7c7-5092-a6ad-ea8f0b72889f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,057" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a98c440-68b6-575b-8ef4-c721edded79e" :answer/id #uuid "3a98c440-68b6-575b-8ef4-c721edded79e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,084" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94519370-0c28-59dc-bc52-6f17306f1892" :answer/id #uuid "94519370-0c28-59dc-bc52-6f17306f1892" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "948" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93637c4f-d62c-514b-a779-eb0ef0e60eb5" :answer/id #uuid "93637c4f-d62c-514b-a779-eb0ef0e60eb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "982" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aac7d9dd-e1f6-55a0-8d3e-a363440ea2a6" :question/id #uuid "aac7d9dd-e1f6-55a0-8d3e-a363440ea2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-159902" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(5,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=5,\\,{x}_{2}=3,\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=5$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{5-x},\\,x=0,1,…,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{5-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(5-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 10\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =100,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=5+3+2=10.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(100)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(5\\cdot 3-10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(100)+10\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=100+10+5=115.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-33039f95-3064-5de3-9d9f-d7b2dc9361eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-33039f95-3064-5de3-9d9f-d7b2dc9361eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "33039f95-3064-5de3-9d9f-d7b2dc9361eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a6ced6e0-f97e-5c2c-9cc2-8f7ae6c7d108" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a8020f1a-ca93-54d7-ad54-028ee4af07b5" "answer-a6ced6e0-f97e-5c2c-9cc2-8f7ae6c7d108" "answer-d0478cc5-b8ff-5c81-a9e5-c9721f21e934" "answer-2894825b-6a43-5769-8dc0-c8fc5328c196" "answer-41537903-4d50-5d6d-bc3d-e57fd2c9c872"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8020f1a-ca93-54d7-ad54-028ee4af07b5" :answer/id #uuid "a8020f1a-ca93-54d7-ad54-028ee4af07b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "105" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6ced6e0-f97e-5c2c-9cc2-8f7ae6c7d108" :answer/id #uuid "a6ced6e0-f97e-5c2c-9cc2-8f7ae6c7d108" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "115" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0478cc5-b8ff-5c81-a9e5-c9721f21e934" :answer/id #uuid "d0478cc5-b8ff-5c81-a9e5-c9721f21e934" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2894825b-6a43-5769-8dc0-c8fc5328c196" :answer/id #uuid "2894825b-6a43-5769-8dc0-c8fc5328c196" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "128" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41537903-4d50-5d6d-bc3d-e57fd2c9c872" :answer/id #uuid "41537903-4d50-5d6d-bc3d-e57fd2c9c872" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "137" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-39034469-ded2-572d-9811-0121a992e366" :question/id #uuid "39034469-ded2-572d-9811-0121a992e366" :question/math-academy-id "q-159890" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(4,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=3.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x},\\,x=0,1,2,3,4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=4$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x},\\,x=0,1,2,3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{4}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1})\\cdot (\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 6\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =384,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nS=1+2+1+3=7.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta )$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(384)+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4\\cdot 4-7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡384+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=384+7+9=400.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cb00ed38-5141-52cf-9136-8d37b807205f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cb00ed38-5141-52cf-9136-8d37b807205f" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb00ed38-5141-52cf-9136-8d37b807205f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-00ba373d-cc1a-530b-a296-6e2025248701" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89d6e489-adf5-5cf8-b171-9aee8a4dc3fb" "answer-07e14f65-82c6-59d4-91a0-6cec2c90bfff" "answer-00ba373d-cc1a-530b-a296-6e2025248701" "answer-e7ef90ce-0ff4-5e3c-b910-fda65e0f5c04" "answer-32e49543-5cf5-59b5-be50-40b7d827aa2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89d6e489-adf5-5cf8-b171-9aee8a4dc3fb" :answer/id #uuid "89d6e489-adf5-5cf8-b171-9aee8a4dc3fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "380" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07e14f65-82c6-59d4-91a0-6cec2c90bfff" :answer/id #uuid "07e14f65-82c6-59d4-91a0-6cec2c90bfff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "385" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00ba373d-cc1a-530b-a296-6e2025248701" :answer/id #uuid "00ba373d-cc1a-530b-a296-6e2025248701" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7ef90ce-0ff4-5e3c-b910-fda65e0f5c04" :answer/id #uuid "e7ef90ce-0ff4-5e3c-b910-fda65e0f5c04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "415" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32e49543-5cf5-59b5-be50-40b7d827aa2c" :answer/id #uuid "32e49543-5cf5-59b5-be50-40b7d827aa2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "420" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d978fa3e-6005-512f-876c-9fe43876b042" :question/id #uuid "d978fa3e-6005-512f-876c-9fe43876b042" :question/math-academy-id "q-159845" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=4.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =1!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 0!\\cdot 4!=1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 24=24, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1+1+0+4=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-4\\theta +6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+6+24=26.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a48845ac-16bf-54fa-96e4-0933e3531df9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a48845ac-16bf-54fa-96e4-0933e3531df9" :answer-field/id #uuid "a48845ac-16bf-54fa-96e4-0933e3531df9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type