ibuted discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=3$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =3!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 2!=6\\cdot 6\\cdot 2=72, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3+3+2=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-3\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-3+8+72=77.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6df3f188-93fa-57a8-a9f9-cc6be388f8c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6df3f188-93fa-57a8-a9f9-cc6be388f8c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6df3f188-93fa-57a8-a9f9-cc6be388f8c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-26378f4c-2152-5e0e-ba87-0cec2f7febdc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9c40038c-0861-5bda-a994-e9dd2d1ed1e7" "answer-a8389079-b7ba-53a7-aa1f-3d18031f7581" "answer-8e5663ed-f2a1-58f3-9568-27128d2426ae" "answer-26378f4c-2152-5e0e-ba87-0cec2f7febdc" "answer-1d47b835-ad4c-501a-9bf3-e4f4532ec9be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c40038c-0861-5bda-a994-e9dd2d1ed1e7" :answer/id #uuid "9c40038c-0861-5bda-a994-e9dd2d1ed1e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8389079-b7ba-53a7-aa1f-3d18031f7581" :answer/id #uuid "a8389079-b7ba-53a7-aa1f-3d18031f7581" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "41" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e5663ed-f2a1-58f3-9568-27128d2426ae" :answer/id #uuid "8e5663ed-f2a1-58f3-9568-27128d2426ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26378f4c-2152-5e0e-ba87-0cec2f7febdc" :answer/id #uuid "26378f4c-2152-5e0e-ba87-0cec2f7febdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "77" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d47b835-ad4c-501a-9bf3-e4f4532ec9be" :answer/id #uuid "1d47b835-ad4c-501a-9bf3-e4f4532ec9be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "83" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9585d3de-f669-5f4a-af08-1394e7187c1e" :question/id #uuid "9585d3de-f669-5f4a-af08-1394e7187c1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159840" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=0,\\,{x}_{5}=2.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =0!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 0!\\cdot 2!=1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2=2, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =0+1+1+0+2=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-5\\theta +4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-5+4+2=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a88b271e-3f4c-50f4-b81d-a23474066dc3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a88b271e-3f4c-50f4-b81d-a23474066dc3" :answer-field/id #uuid "a88b271e-3f4c-50f4-b81d-a23474066dc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b573e814-3957-5587-9de1-3a2e818a8fb8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b573e814-3957-5587-9de1-3a2e818a8fb8" "answer-d4d1affc-643d-5b02-b0b9-9322e0fbfbfb" "answer-94bca7df-8b14-5bdf-853c-62f3c1725aa9" "answer-2c2d2438-0c3d-5904-bc13-d7b69489bdc5" "answer-0b42731d-ab12-5439-bd94-262e8f0cb47d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b573e814-3957-5587-9de1-3a2e818a8fb8" :answer/id #uuid "b573e814-3957-5587-9de1-3a2e818a8fb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4d1affc-643d-5b02-b0b9-9322e0fbfbfb" :answer/id #uuid "d4d1affc-643d-5b02-b0b9-9322e0fbfbfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94bca7df-8b14-5bdf-853c-62f3c1725aa9" :answer/id #uuid "94bca7df-8b14-5bdf-853c-62f3c1725aa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c2d2438-0c3d-5904-bc13-d7b69489bdc5" :answer/id #uuid "2c2d2438-0c3d-5904-bc13-d7b69489bdc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b42731d-ab12-5439-bd94-262e8f0cb47d" :answer/id #uuid "0b42731d-ab12-5439-bd94-262e8f0cb47d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-feffea6d-5c4c-51cd-843c-44b1eafaf816" :question/id #uuid "feffea6d-5c4c-51cd-843c-44b1eafaf816" :question/math-academy-id "q-159848" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=0,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=5,\\,{x}_{4}=1.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =0!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 5!\\cdot 1!=1\\cdot 2\\cdot 120\\cdot 1=240, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =0+2+5+1=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-4\\theta +8\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡240.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+8+240=244.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-84290a67-87f1-59fd-a0d4-ccc92cc9e83d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-84290a67-87f1-59fd-a0d4-ccc92cc9e83d" :answer-field/id #uuid "84290a67-87f1-59fd-a0d4-ccc92cc9e83d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c6d9ceb0-677d-51bf-b30c-3a0dcc272add" :answer-field/choices ["answer-689640bd-429f-5183-abe7-85451782e800" "answer-df65b4d4-23b7-5403-91fc-e90db7db8f28" "answer-cf87a566-ae15-5625-968b-e6c425040aa7" "answer-c6d9ceb0-677d-51bf-b30c-3a0dcc272add" "answer-29de9cdd-8677-581d-8f42-fb4b32f9c8a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-689640bd-429f-5183-abe7-85451782e800" :answer/id #uuid "689640bd-429f-5183-abe7-85451782e800" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "124" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df65b4d4-23b7-5403-91fc-e90db7db8f28" :answer/id #uuid "df65b4d4-23b7-5403-91fc-e90db7db8f28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "166" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf87a566-ae15-5625-968b-e6c425040aa7" :answer/id #uuid "cf87a566-ae15-5625-968b-e6c425040aa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "228" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6d9ceb0-677d-51bf-b30c-3a0dcc272add" :answer/id #uuid "c6d9ceb0-677d-51bf-b30c-3a0dcc272add" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "244" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29de9cdd-8677-581d-8f42-fb4b32f9c8a0" :answer/id #uuid "29de9cdd-8677-581d-8f42-fb4b32f9c8a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "262" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-680ba03b-6073-56c3-bf2a-b6b3fb6a93d2" :question/id #uuid "680ba03b-6073-56c3-bf2a-b6b3fb6a93d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159847" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ with unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=3,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=5.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=a\\theta +b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡c.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{x}}{x!}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}}{{x}_{i}!}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{-\\theta })+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(-\\theta )+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\theta )-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{i}!) \\\\  & =-n\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}!)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the sample data with $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}! & =3!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 5!=6\\cdot 2\\cdot 1\\cdot 120=1\\,440, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =3+2+1+5=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting the above values into our expression for $l(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =-4\\theta +11\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡1\\,440.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=-4+11+1\\,440=1\\,447.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-22f32989-72b2-5437-9672-f47842734fbb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-22f32989-72b2-5437-9672-f47842734fbb" :answer-field/id #uuid "22f32989-72b2-5437-9672-f47842734fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-725d6c8e-de45-5da8-bf6d-00039ba4a5a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-725d6c8e-de45-5da8-bf6d-00039ba4a5a2" "answer-61625b6e-fafc-5735-99c7-51bacdd3efaa" "answer-5b6624e9-ff65-58e4-bb1a-cbc97c080b3a" "answer-6729961d-5125-51e3-9441-2b4cea93a506" "answer-5227129a-cf10-5014-b945-9b282b56a162"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-725d6c8e-de45-5da8-bf6d-00039ba4a5a2" :answer/id #uuid "725d6c8e-de45-5da8-bf6d-00039ba4a5a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,447" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61625b6e-fafc-5735-99c7-51bacdd3efaa" :answer/id #uuid "61625b6e-fafc-5735-99c7-51bacdd3efaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,465" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b6624e9-ff65-58e4-bb1a-cbc97c080b3a" :answer/id #uuid "5b6624e9-ff65-58e4-bb1a-cbc97c080b3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "432" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6729961d-5125-51e3-9441-2b4cea93a506" :answer/id #uuid "6729961d-5125-51e3-9441-2b4cea93a506" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "727" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5227129a-cf10-5014-b945-9b282b56a162" :answer/id #uuid "5227129a-cf10-5014-b945-9b282b56a162" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "743" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193898 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $X_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, \\: x_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: x_n$ is \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nFor example, if \n\n$$\nX_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3,\\quad X_4,\\quad X_5,\\quad X_6\n$$\n\nis an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is the unknown probability of success, then the likelihood function of the sample \n\n$$\nx_1=0, \\quad x_2=1, \\quad x_3=1, \\quad x_4=0, \\quad x_5=1, \\quad x_6=1\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^{4}(1-\\theta)^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe likelihood function contains lots of products, which can be cumbersome to work with. To make things easier, we convert the products into sums by finding the natural logarithm of $L(\\theta),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(L(\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta )\\cdot f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta )⋯f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{1};\\,\\theta ))+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{2};\\,\\theta ))+⋯+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{n};\\,\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis new function $l(\\theta)$ is called the **log-likelihood function** of the sample.\n\nFor our Bernoulli example above, the corresponding log-likelihood function is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{4}(1-\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186193901 :tutorial/content "The graph of the likelihood function \n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\theta^4(1\\!-\\!\\theta)^2\n$$\n\nfor the Bernoulli distribution we saw earlier is shown below. \n\n![](images/28/28c73749a566d5531ef2f13c5b01bd1d26ced28e19749abf056841492fb497f5.png)\n\nThe graph of the corresponding log-likelihood function\n\n$$\nl(\\theta) = 4 \\ln \\theta + 2 \\ln(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f7f0ba3147504869e162dd371f98d5d6c492e89345f80c75037bdc82a6006d1c.png)\n\nAlthough the graphs look quite different, they both have a maximum at the same point $\\theta = \\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}.$ This will become important later. \n\nLet's see more examples of finding the log-likelihood functions for different distributions and samples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186192933 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
